>>>>> "Lennart" == Lennart Poettering <mz6e657466696c746572@itaparica.org> writes:
    Lennart> packet of 1500 bytes would result in a packet of 1500 bytes and
    Lennart> another of 20+ip-header-length. However, this will slow down the
    Lennart> things massively.

    Lennart> Is there any sane way to get this fixed correctly?

  Sane? No :-)

  Seriously, see RFC2003 as well as the DF and PMTU sections of RFC2401
for some thoughts. Are you adding a new IP header? Or is something else
happening?

  You need to send an ICMP fragment needed to the sender of the packet.

    Lennart> I would like to see a second MTU-value attached to each network
    Lennart> device which specifies a higher limit than the normal
    Lennart> MTU. Packets which are larger than this second MTU get dropped,
    Lennart> packets which are larger than the normal MTU but smaller than
    Lennart> that special MTU are accepted. So that I would be able to set
    Lennart> the normal mtu to 1480 and the effective MTU to 1500 and be
    Lennart> happy this way. Is there anything like this available in the
    Lennart> Linux kernel currently?

  Yes, you can specify the MTU as part of a route, and as part of the 
neighbour cache. The later gets created automatically, so might take some
code to do what you want. 

    Lennart> Maybe there is a simple sysctl variable that may modify the
    Lennart> packet size the kernel splits its TCP stream with?

  If you are strictly dealing with locally generated packets, then it should
be easier. A virtual interface is one way to deal with this, but is has other
less desireable effects as well.

